H2: Why Some Brokers Still Choose MT4
It's fair to ask why anyone would launch on MT4 when MT5 exists. The answer usually comes down to clients.
MT4 built one of the largest trading communities in retail forex. Thousands of Expert Advisors, custom indicators and scripts were written for it in MQL4, and plenty of traders still depend on those tools. Moving to MT5 means those tools need to be rewritten, which many traders aren't willing to do.
MT4 is also simpler. It was designed around forex trading, and for traders who only trade currency pairs, that focus is a feature rather than a limitation. Its interface is familiar, its behaviour is predictable and its hedging model is one many forex traders are used to.
So if your target market includes experienced forex traders, EA users, copy-trading communities or regions where MT4 remains the default, offering MT4 can make a real difference to how quickly you win clients.
H2: What Is an MT4 White Label?
An MT4 white label is a branded MetaTrader 4 environment set up for your brokerage. Clients trade on a terminal that carries your name. Your team manages trading groups, account types, symbols and conditions through administration tools, with the underlying platform technology provided and supported for you.
Compared with a grey label, a white label generally gives you greater control over how the trading environment is configured and run. You decide how account types are structured, how leverage and margin rules are applied and how orders are handled, and you can adjust those settings as your business changes.
There's one practical point to understand before you plan an MT4 brokerage setup. MetaQuotes, the developer of MetaTrader, has shifted its focus heavily towards MT5, and new MT4 availability is more limited than it once was. What's possible depends on the provider arrangement and your circumstances. Part of our job is to give you a straight answer on what's realistically available for your business, rather than letting you plan around something that may not be on offer.
As with any platform arrangement, an MT4 white label is technology. It isn't a licence to operate as a broker. Regulatory requirements vary by jurisdiction.
H2: How It Works
An MT4 forex broker runs on the same basic chain as any modern brokerage.
Your MT4 trading server holds your groups, symbols, trading conditions and client accounts. It's hosted on monitored infrastructure with backups in place.
A liquidity bridge connects the server to your liquidity providers. Prices flow in, client orders flow out according to your routing rules. Because MT4 was built for a different era of connectivity, a well-configured bridge matters here. It handles the translation between MT4 and modern liquidity infrastructure, including symbol mapping and execution reporting.
Your forex CRM connects to MT4 so trading accounts, balances and client data stay aligned. Payment providers and KYC tools link into the CRM.
Your website and client portal sit at the front, bringing traders in, verifying them, funding their accounts and giving them access to MT4 under your brand.
